Use the clz builtins whenever the compiler has them

FIRSTBITNZ counts the leading zeros of a nonzero setword. Two of the three
implementations of it are guarded by HAVE_HWLZCNT, which configure sets when it
believes the target CPU has the lzcnt instruction.

That guard is needed for the MSVC branch, which calls _lzcnt_u64 and so really
does emit lzcnt. Getting it wrong there is silent: lzcnt is encoded as REP BSR,
so on a CPU without it the prefix is ignored, bsr runs instead and returns a
different number rather than faulting.

The gcc and clang branch has no such requirement. __builtin_clzll is an
operation, not an instruction, and the compiler picks an encoding the target
actually has: bsr on baseline x86, clz on ARM, cntlzd on PowerPC. lzcnt itself
is only emitted with -mlzcnt, -mabm or -mbmi, none of which nauty ever passes.
So gate that branch on HAVE_CLZLL and friends, which record whether the compiler
accepts the builtins, and leave the MSVC branch as it is.

The argument is nonzero at every call site, which is what the NZ in the name
means, so the builtins are never called with the value they leave undefined.

--- a/nauty-h.in
+++ b/nauty-h.in
@@ -980,7 +980,6 @@
 
 #else  /* Not MAGMA or WIN */
 
-#if HAVE_HWLZCNT
 #if defined(SETWORD_LONGLONG) && HAVE_CLZLL
 #define FIRSTBITNZ(x) __builtin_clzll(x)
 #elif defined(SETWORD_LONG) && HAVE_CLZL
@@ -994,7 +993,6 @@
    __builtin_clzll((unsigned long long)((x)>>64)) : \
    64 + __builtin_clzll((unsigned long long)((x)&MASK128R)))
 #endif /* size choice */
-#endif /* HAVE_HWLZCNT */
 
 #endif /* MAGMA-WIN-OTHER choice */
 
